A||||7||mrtgman||Need electric door lock help!||||8||
Z||000000||mrtgman||09-27-2001||12:05 PM||||My 86 Gt has been at my mechanics for a week now. He is trying to figure out why the electric door locks will not lock or unlock with only one push of the button. We have replaced the actuators with brand new ones, greased everything up really good and still no luck! Any ideas?<P>Thanks.||||reg||8
Z||000001||MinnGreenGT||09-27-2001||12:57 PM||||I'm having the same problem, but just with the passenger side.  I'm getting a new solinoid from Doni to try.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JSocha||09-27-2001||01:09 PM||||Regarding you locking your door with one push of the button:<P>Are the door panels on or off when you try this?<P>If the door panels are off and you still have to hit the button several times, make sure the mesh net around the rods are floating freely, not rubbing up against the door and not snagging on the rod.<P>My '87 GT had the same problem after I bought the car last November.  <P>After taking off the door panels, cleaning and lubricating all the respective joints with WD40, I hit the door lock/unlock switch several times and they moved to the locked and unlocked position with no problems.  <P>However, once I put the door panels back on, I once again had to hit the button several times to get the locks to move to either the fully locked/unlocked position.  Over time, my drivers door moves to the locked/unlocked position on the first press.  <P>My passenger door needs to come off again as I know the links are possibly still sticking.<P>Make sure you clean your interior door handles and lubricate them along with the plastic lock slide really, really good.  I stuck my driver side lock slider in the dishwasher with a load of dishes (side I'm having no problems with).  Then I put a shot of SILCOLN spray lubricant on them.<P>The passenger side I washed the lock slider by hand and didn't put any spray on it as I was out.  I'm going to be removing that first and throwing that in the dishwasher and lubricating that one again before I take the door panel off to see if that helps.<P>I'll let you know what happens with my '86GT once I get mine back from the mechanic and its locks as I'm going to be removing those door panels in a couple of weeks from now when I get some other parts I ordered, cleaning and lubricating everything on that car to get those locks to work.  Passenger side does not move.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||3
Z||000003||mrtgman||09-27-2001||01:59 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MinnGreenGT:<BR><B>I'm having the same problem, but just with the passenger side.  I'm getting a new solinoid from Doni to try.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is a solinoid (solinoide) different from an actuator?<p>[This message has been edited by mrtgman (edited 09-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrtgman||09-27-2001||07:22 PM||||bump for foxgapfiero||||reg||1
Z||000005||JSocha||10-08-2001||12:04 PM||||How is your lock problem going?<P>Took my 86GT door panels apart this weekend.<P>Clean and lubricate all rods, joints with SILICOLN spray that is related to both the lock and door latch, including the actuator that moves the thingy-mah-jigy back and fourth.  WD40 the door bolt latch after cleaning it thourghly.<P>Under the paper, there is a small clip holding the lock rod to the door.  Pop the rod out carefully (so as not to break the clip if brittle), clean the clip and lubricate it with SILICOLN spray along with the rod in that area.  Snap rod back in carefully.  <P>If this rod is rough as mine was, take some emmery cloth and smooth out the rod a bit in that area and spray it with silicoln.<P>Do a few test runs of locking/unlocking with your door panel off.  Lubricate more if necessary.  You should get full movement both ways.<P>Put your door latch bezzle and the lock slide in your dishwasher (gets both of them clean  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) before putting your door panel back on.<P>Reinstall the door panel.<P>My 86GT's lock on the passenger side now moves (and fully I might add) in both directions in one push.  Did it with the passenger side of my 87 as well this weekend.  That door is cured as well!  That black lock rod clip under the paper was the culprit along with a rough textured rod.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||gomobile||10-08-2001||03:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MinnGreenGT:<BR><B>I'm having the same problem, but just with the passenger side.  I'm getting a new solinoid from Doni to try.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I had the same problem took of the panels as I also had to change the DEWS. Anyway did all the cleaning and such. Oh yeah I did use White Grease for the pesky items (arms) that move back and forth. No more problems after that. ||||reg||6
Z||000007||LivoniaFieroGT||10-08-2001||08:43 PM||||What is commonly called a solenoid is called an actuator by GM in the build process and parts book 22P.  You can manually rough check the actuator by giving resistance (hold in place) and activating it.  If it gives a reasonable snap out or in it is strong enough to push well lubed, clean and unrestricted linkage.||||reg||6
